//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// A region placed on the host with a loop in it. `spawn on(Topology::CPU)`
// lowers to `async.execute`, whose body the async dialect requires to be a
// single block -- and a `for` is four. So the region cannot be carried across
// as written, and the compiler says which construct is the problem.
//
// Before, `convert-vx-to-standard` moved the first block's operations into the
// async body and dropped every other block, leaving the branch to them stranded
// mid-body. That produced invalid IR, reported as
//
//   operation with block successors must terminate its parent block
//
// which names neither the region nor the loop, and points at an operation the
// program never wrote. The pass now moves the region whole, so a shape the
// async dialect cannot hold is rejected here, before any of it is rewritten.
//
// This is a limitation of the host lowering, not of placement: the same loop in
// a region placed on a device is outlined into a kernel, keeps its blocks, and
// compiles -- tests/backend/pass/spliced_block_tails.vx does exactly that.
//
// Note that a host region is not runnable today for a second and unrelated
// reason: nothing lowers `async.execute`, and `mlir-translate` does not have
// the async dialect registered, so even a single-block one fails to translate.
// That is a separate gap and this diagnostic does not paper over it -- the two
// have different causes and different fixes.
//
// RUN: not vxc %s --action emit-llvm 2>&1 | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: cannot carry control flow
// CHECK: async.execute
// CHECK: place the region on a device topology

fn main() -> i32 {
  let mut a = Tensor<f32>([ 1, 1 ]);
  a[0][0] = 0.0;
  spawn on(Topology::CPU) {
    for i in 0..3 {
      a[0][0] = a[0][0] + 1.0;
    }
  }
  print(a);
  return 0;
}
